<https://example.org/kg/resource/comment/TEST-12/0> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/title> "TEST-12: We decided to use a JSON file for the golden table with keys in the format `part.nt` rather than bare part names. This matches the filename convention in snapshot/parts/ and makes the table directly readable as a map from filename to line count. JSON is already parsed by the Python stdlib w"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<https://example.org/kg/resource/comment/TEST-12/0> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://example.org/kg/onto#Decision> .
<https://example.org/kg/resource/comment/TEST-12/0> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasAttributedTo> <https://example.org/kg/resource/person/eve> .
<https://example.org/kg/resource/comment/TEST-12/0> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom> <https://example.org/kg/resource/issue/TEST-12> .
<https://example.org/kg/resource/comment/TEST-12/0> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasGeneratedBy> <https://example.org/kg/resource/run/golden-run> .
<https://example.org/kg/resource/comment/TEST-12/0> <https://example.org/kg/onto#detail> "We decided to use a JSON file for the golden table with keys in the format `part.nt` rather than bare part names. This matches the filename convention in snapshot/parts/ and makes the table directly readable as a map from filename to line count. JSON is already parsed by the Python stdlib without any additional dependency, it round-trips cleanly through json.dumps with sort_keys=True and indent=2, and it is already familiar to contributors who read the tracker-data fixture. YAML would require PyYAML which is not in requirements.txt. CSV would complicate the key structure and require a custom parser. The JSON approach produces the simplest possible diff when the golden changes: each line is exactly one key-value pair, so a change to one snapshot part shows as exactly one changed line in the pull request diff." .
<https://example.org/kg/resource/comment/TEST-12/0> <https://example.org/kg/onto#tagged> <https://example.org/kg/resource/topic/architecture> .
